> Doug Freyburger <dfrey...@yahoo.com> wrote:
>
>> and write up a skill slot strategy
>
> I don't always do that, but maybe I should.  I've been
> operating on the principle of "play it by ear" under
> the theory that I don't know what weapons will be
> available until I find them -- so I make my list of
> what I can get skills in, then I (curse-test and)
> pick up and use the best thing I find, trading up
> when I find something better.

Some skill slots are worth doing at basic - There are several good long
sword artifacts.  Some skill slots are not worth enhancing even if you
could end up expert in them - There are no artifact short swords and
they are inferior to long sowrds for melee and daggers for missile
combat.

Mostly I find it to be a compromise between what weapons types I know I
will find, what artifacts I hope to gather, what I can get past basic,
what I think is a dev team trap to waste valuable resources.

> I've been operating under the assumption
> that spell categories are worth advancing
> to basic even if I can't go any farther, if
> there's even one spell in the class that I
> cast often enough to be able to do so.
>
> (Generally speaking, the only spells I
> can imagine casting often enough to
> advance a skill are light, identify, healing
> and its higher-level brethren, and possibly
> remove curse.  But I haven't yet played a
> lot in heavy spell-casting roles (e.g.,
> Wizard), so when I get around to that
> I may need to alter my strategy.  Most
> of the roles I've played can advance to
> basic skill in at most three spell schools,
> at least one of which contains no useful
> low-level spells.)

Do play more roles.  On the other hand play wizards for a while and
you'll learn that skilled in some schools is plenty.  Level, luck and
armor effects matter.  Once you can cast every spell in a school at 0%
failure it no longer helps to enhance that school any more.  Once I
learned that I started enahncing my wizards in weapon schools other than
dagger, broadsword and rarely quarterstaff.  But mostly because I figure
if I have the slots to spend I may as well do so.

